fact WorkItem(id: "WI-831", description: some(value: "anthill-todo: move persisted work-item relations to a durable extent owner. Replace the resident-mirror read path for WorkItem, Tag, and Feedback with an indexed file-backed owner that queries rows lazily (enumerable plus keyed modes), returns Value/StoredRow without creating RuleEntry or hash-consed heads per row, and uses store-native FactRef/RowKey rather than RuleId→source-span identity. Keep domain/workflow bodied rules resident; reject mixed resident facts for owned functors. Preserve existing CLI semantics for list, tagged/unblocked queries, add/claim/update/retract/feedback, and source-editable workitems.anthill. Depends on the extent write/FactRef cutover (WI-780) and follows WI-830's explicit mirror-role migration; do not treat the existing IndexedFileStore (whose retrieve reads kb.rules) as an owner without redesigning it."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-780", "WI-830"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-831", name: "extent-seam")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-831", author: "user", content: "RE-READ 2026-08-17 against the WI-437 track, which changed this ticket's substrate under it. Still wanted -- more so -- but cheaper in one way, harder in another, and now sequenced.\n\nTHE MOTIVATION, WHICH THIS TICKET NEVER STATED. Measured on the live tracker today, debug build: 'anthill-todo list' takes ~9.5s, and it is paid on EVERY invocation -- claim, show, feedback, all of it -- because every row is loaded resident before anything is answered. 4677 lines / 5.2MB, 1112 items. A release build is several times faster, but the shape is the point: the cost is per-invocation and scales with tracker size, not with the query.\n\nRELATION TO WI-437: ORTHOGONAL AXES ON THE SAME LAYER, which is why this is tagged extent-seam and not wi437 and should stay that way. WI-437 is WHICH BACKEND (the write side and the on-disk layout). This is WHICH ROLE -- proposal 057's owner vs mirror, the READ side: does the KB hold every row, or does the store answer queries lazily? But they point straight at each other:\n\n(1) WI-437's DELIVERED CODE CONTAINS THIS TICKET'S BLOCKER, DELIBERATELY. register_declared_store (anthill-todo/src/main.rs, WI-830/WI-1114) hard-refuses role: owner() with 'anthill-todo loads every work item at startup and answers reads from the KB, so its store is a durability mirror; an owner would have to serve reads from its own query engine. Write role: mirror().' Removing that refusal IS this ticket.\n\n(2) WI-1114 MADE THIS MUCH CHEAPER -- THE FILESYSTEM BECAME THE INDEX. This ticket was written against the single-file store, where 'an indexed file-backed owner that queries rows lazily (enumerable plus keyed modes)' means building a byte-range index into a 5MB file. Under file-per-item (delivered) a KEYED lookup is a PATH and an ENUMERABLE-by-status read is a READDIR on one directory. The index this ticket asks for mostly already exists as the directory tree.\n\n(3) AND IT MADE THIS MORE URGENT, which is the part to underline. File-per-item plus resident-everything means ~1112 file opens and 1112 parses per invocation where today it is one large read for the same 9.5s. The layout change and the role change are load-bearing for each other: file-per-item is what makes lazy reads natural, and lazy reads are what keep file-per-item's load affordable.\n\nFRICTION -- WI-1114 BUILT THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT AN OWNER NEEDS. ItemPerFileStore deliberately implements NO retrieve and declares no QueryableStore fact ('it keeps no index a pattern could be answered from, and a scan over its block model would be a linear walk wearing an index's name'), and it holds TEXT -- a per-file block model -- not parsed rows, while an owner must return Value/StoredRow. This ticket's own caveat ('do not treat the existing IndexedFileStore, whose retrieve reads kb.rules, as an owner without redesigning it') now applies to the NEW store too. Expect to add a real query path, not to reuse one.\n\nALSO: the bundle reads through facts_of(kb(), WorkItem) in every WorkItemStore body (anthill-todo/anthill/store.anthill), so the read path is resident by construction on the anthill side as well, not just the host side.\n\nDEPENDENCY ADDED ON WI-1120 (work items are documents): once the item file is an anthill head plus markdown chapters, a lazy owner must parse THAT to answer a keyed read. Building the owner before the format settles means building it twice -- the same argument that put WI-1120 ahead of WI-1118.\n\nNO EDGE ON WI-1121 (the id shape), DELIBERATELY, so nobody adds one later: an owner's keyed read is by full id (the filename stem) and its enumerable read is a readdir, and both work for any id shape. WI-1121's fragment-resolution ladder is a CLI concern that resolves a fragment to an id BEFORE the owner is asked -- and where it needs to enumerate, that is the enumerable mode this ticket already provides. NO EDGE ON WI-1114 either: it is Delivered, so an edge would convey nothing actionable; the relationship is recorded here instead.", at: "2026-08-17T09:57:22Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-831", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION 2026-08-17, same day: the ~9.5s figure in the note above is WRONG -- it was a DEBUG build, measured on a machine still busy from a test suite, in violation of this repo's own benchmark-on-a-quiet-machine rule. Re-measured, best of 3:\n\n  debug     9.67s\n  release   1.41s\n  installed 1.65s   (~/.cargo/bin/anthill-todo, which is what anyone actually runs)\n\nAND THE DECOMPOSITION MATTERS MORE THAN THE TOTAL. Timing an EMPTY project (project.anthill only, zero work items) against the live tracker, release build:\n\n  empty project (stdlib + bundle only)   0.97s\n  real tracker (1112 items, 5.2MB)       1.41s\n\nSO THE 1112 WORK ITEMS COST ~0.44s OF 1.41s. The fixed stdlib+bundle load is 0.97s -- 69% of the time -- and this ticket cannot touch it. An extent OWNER makes ITEM reads lazy; it does nothing about loading the stdlib, the bundle and main.anthill's 3227 lines on every invocation.\n\nTHIS CAPS THE TICKET'S PAYOFF AT ~0.44s, or ~31%, not at the 9.5s the earlier note implied. That is still worth having and the ARCHITECTURAL arguments are untouched -- lazy reads, no resident subtree, store-native FactRef instead of RuleId->span identity, and the file-per-item substrate that makes keyed reads a path and enumerable reads a readdir. But it should be claimed for the design, not for a latency number, and anyone hoping to make 'list' feel instant will be disappointed: the floor is 0.97s until the BUNDLE load is addressed, which is a different ticket and the bigger lever.\n\nMEASUREMENT METHOD, so it can be repeated: /usr/bin/time -p on 'list', best of 3, release build, load average checked first. The empty-project baseline is the important half -- without it a total is uninterpretable.", at: "2026-08-17T10:03:00Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-831", author: "user", content: "DEFINITIVE MEASUREMENT 2026-08-17, superseding BOTH earlier figures on this ticket. The first (9.5s) was a debug build; the second decomposition (items = 31%) was measured NON-INTERLEAVED on a loaded machine, so its empty-project baseline was inflated to 0.97s when the true floor is 0.53s. Both were wrong. This one is interleaved -- all cases run in the same loop so they share machine conditions -- release build, best of 5:\n\n  empty project, stdlib+bundle only            0.53s\n  161 files / 0.57MB  (non-terminal items)     0.73s\n  1114 files / 5.2MB  (every item, load-all)   1.33s\n\nSO: the fixed bundle load is 0.53s and THE 1112 ITEMS COST ~0.80s -- about 60% of list latency, not the 31% the previous note claimed.\n\nWHAT THIS TICKET IS ACTUALLY WORTH: list displays only NON-TERMINAL items (1112 minus 920 Delivered minus 39 Verified = ~153), and those are 161 files holding 0.57MB -- 14% of the files and 11% of the bytes, because the terminal items carry almost all the prose. A lazy owner that loads only what it shows would run list at ~0.73s against today's 1.33s. A 45% cut, and the floor after it is the 0.53s bundle load, which this ticket cannot touch and which is the bigger remaining lever.\n\nNO INDEX FILE IS NEEDED, and this answers the question directly. Reading 161 files costs 0.20s, of which raw I/O is ~6ms (measured: 42ms for all 1114 files) -- the cost is PARSING PROSE, not opening files. An index would replace that 0.20s with maybe 0.02s, so it buys ~0.18s beyond lazy reading, against a 0.53s floor, at the price of duplicated data that must be invalidated. Not worth it. And the part that WOULD have justified an index is already free: the blocked/unblocked computation needs only id and STATUS for every item, and under WI-1114 both are in the PATH -- id is the filename stem, status is the directory. A readdir over nine directories indexes the whole tracker with zero file reads and zero duplication. THE DIRECTORY TREE IS THE INDEX.\n\nALSO MEASURED, and it retires a worry: file-per-item is latency-NEUTRAL for the load-all path. 1114 files 1.38s vs the single 5.2MB file 1.30s, interleaved -- about 6% for 1114 extra opens, consistent with the 42ms raw I/O. Splitting the tracker does not make loading it slower, so WI-1114 costs nothing here and WI-831 is free to be claimed for its own reasons.\n\nMETHOD, so this is repeatable and so the earlier mistakes are not: interleave the cases in one loop, take best-of-5, use a RELEASE build, check load averages first, and always measure an EMPTY-project baseline -- a total without it is uninterpretable, which is exactly how the two earlier numbers went wrong.", at: "2026-08-17T10:14:05Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-831", author: "user", content: "EDGE ON WI-1120 REMOVED 2026-08-17 -- it was a SEQUENCING judgement, not a technical prerequisite, and this repo's rule is that edges are prerequisites only because an edge blocks claiming. WI-831 and WI-1120 CAN run in parallel and be merged, under two conditions.\n\nCONDITION 1 -- ISOLATE THE PER-FILE READ BEHIND ONE FUNCTION. The entire overlap between these two tickets is a single seam: 'given a path, produce the facts in it'. WI-1120 DEFINES it (find the fenced anthill head, parse the prose chapters into Feedback facts); WI-831 CALLS it, lazily, once per keyed or enumerated read. If WI-831 inlines parse::parse at several call sites the merge becomes a scatter; if it routes every per-file read through one function, the merge is repointing that function at WI-1120's version. Everything else is disjoint -- WI-1120 touches the loader glob, the block model and the stage0 mapping facts; WI-831 touches the role refusal in register_declared_store, a query path on the store, and the WorkItemStore read bodies in the bundle. Co-location in item_per_file_store.rs may produce textual conflicts; they are not semantic ones.\n\nCONDITION 2 -- TARGET ItemPerFileStore, NOT IndexedFileStore. Building the owner over the single-file store would be building it for a layout the tracker is leaving, and it would not merge with WI-1120 at all, since WI-1120 only touches the per-item format. Develop against fixtures with -d <tmpdir>, exactly as WI-1114 did; this repo's own tracker does not need to have migrated first.\n\nAND WI-1120 MAKES THIS TICKET BETTER, not merely compatible -- worth knowing before choosing an order. Once the item file is a small structured HEAD plus a large prose BODY, a lazy read for status or description can stop at the closing fence and never touch the chapters. That is where the bytes are: measured, the terminal items carry ~89% of the tracker's 5.2MB, and prose is ~92% of it. So the head/body split is exactly the shape a lazy owner wants. If anything, doing WI-1120 first makes WI-831 cheaper AND faster; doing them in parallel just costs the one seam merge.", at: "2026-08-17T10:18:56Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-831", author: "user", content: "RECOMMENDED ORDER: WI-1120 FIRST. Parallel remains legal under the two conditions above -- this is a recommendation, not an edge, which is why no dependency was re-added. But the reason is stronger than avoiding a merge, and it partly corrects what the previous note said.\n\nWI-1120 CHANGES WHAT THE OPTIMAL LAZY READ IS. Today an item is ONE SYNTACTIC UNIT: fact WorkItem(id:, description:, ..., status:). To learn an item's status you must parse its entire description -- and §5.3 measured descriptions plus feedback content at 92.5% of the tracker's bytes (4,729,493 of 5,115,651 characters). After WI-1120 the status lives in a small fenced head and the prose is separate chapters, so a reader can answer 'what is the status of X' without touching the prose at all. That is a structural enabler for laziness that DOES NOT EXIST TODAY.\n\nSo building this ticket first means designing its read strategy against the flat form -- parse the whole file to get one field -- and then wanting to REDESIGN it rather than repoint one function. The previous note understated the merge cost in that direction.\n\nAND IT MAY ABSORB MUCH OF THIS TICKET'S LATENCY CASE, which is worth knowing BEFORE scoping it. If heads are ~7.5% of the bytes, reading every item's head is a small fraction of the measured 0.80s that the items currently cost. Whether that lands as 'the owner is no longer needed for speed' or 'the owner is now cheap to write' depends on how selective the WI-1120 reader is, and either way it should be measured after WI-1120 lands rather than assumed now. What is NOT in doubt is that this ticket's remaining case is architectural -- proposal 057's owner role, store-native FactRef instead of RuleId-to-span identity, no resident subtree for owned functors -- and that case is unaffected by any of the numbers.", at: "2026-08-17T10:20:06Z")
